 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dfm0_Whtx_x/",
 "title": "Better opening line for conversations by Chris Voss",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator discusses how to open a conversation using a technique from former FBI negotiator Chris Voss. Instead of asking 'Do you have a few minutes?', the creator suggests asking 'Is now a bad time to talk?' because it switches the dynamic from yes to no and avoids making the other person feel trapped.",
 "key_points": [
  "Most people open conversations with: 'Do you have a few minutes?'",
  "Instead, say: 'Is now a bad time to talk?'",
  "The creator claims everyone has been conditioned to think that trying to get a 'yes' means you are leading them somewhere.",
  "Each 'yes' is a micro agreement or a trap.",
  "Switching to a 'no' question avoids this conditioning."
 ],
 "content": "Most people open with: do you have a few minutes? What do you say instead and why does it work? Is now a bad time to talk? It's again it's switching from yes to no. And the reason it works is everybody has been conditioned that if you're trying to get me to say yes, you're leading me someplace. Each yes is a micro agreement or a tie down. And bang I got you.",
 "status": "done",
